{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"numpy-operations","title":"Array Operations","group":"NumPy","order":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Arithmetic, aggregation, sorting, joining, splitting and filtering — all without writing a loop.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-symbols\"></i>Element-wise arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\na = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\nb = np.array([5, 6, 7, 8])\nprint(a + 2, a * 2, a / 2)        # [3 4 5 6] [2 4 6 8] [0.5 1.  1.5 2. ]\nprint(a + b, b - a)               # [ 6  8 10 12] [4 4 4 4]\nprint(np.add(1, b), np.multiply(a, b))   # [6 7 8 9] [ 5 12 21 32]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Combining an array with a single number (or arrays of compatible shapes) is called <strong>broadcasting</strong> — the number is applied to every element.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sum\"></i>Aggregations and axes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\nm = np.array([[1, 4, 2],\n              [3, 4, 6]])\nprint(m.max(), m.min(), m.sum())      # 6 1 20\nprint(m.max(axis=0))                  # [3 4 6]   ← down each column\nprint(m.max(axis=1))                  # [4 6]     ← across each row\nprint(m.mean(axis=1))                 # [2.33333333 4.33333333]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-down\"></i><code>axis=0</code></h3><p>Collapse the rows — one result <strong>per column</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i><code>axis=1</code></h3><p>Collapse the columns — one result <strong>per row</strong>.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i>Sorting</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\narr = np.array([[1, 4, 2], [3, 4, 6], [0, -1, 5]])\nprint(np.sort(arr))                   # sorts each row (axis=-1 by default)\nprint(np.sort(arr, axis=0))           # sorts each column</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[[ 1  2  4]\n [ 3  4  6]\n [-1  0  5]]\n[[ 0 -1  2]\n [ 1  4  5]\n [ 3  4  6]]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>kind=\"quicksort\" | \"mergesort\" | \"stable\"</code> picks the algorithm; the result is the same, only speed and stability (order of equal elements) differ.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-join\"></i>Joining and splitting</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\na = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\nb = np.array([5, 6, 7, 8])\nprint(np.vstack((a, b)).shape)        # (2, 4)   ← stacked as rows\nprint(np.hstack((a, b)))              # [1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8]\nm = np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\nprint(np.concatenate((m, m), axis=0).shape)   # (4, 2)\ntop, bottom = np.vsplit(np.vstack((a, b)), 2)\nleft, right = np.hsplit(np.vstack((a, b)), 2)\nprint(top, right[1])                  # [[1 2 3 4]] [7 8]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cut\"></i>Slicing and boolean filtering</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\narr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])\nprint(arr[1:5], arr[1:5:2], arr[-3:-1], arr[::2])   # [2 3 4 5] [2 4] [5 6] [1 3 5 7]\n\nnums = np.arange(1, 40)\nprint(nums[nums &gt; 35])                  # [36 37 38 39]\nprint(nums[(nums % 10 == 0)])           # [10 20 30]\nm = np.arange(12).reshape(3, 4)\nprint(m[1], m[:, 2], m[1:, :2].tolist())   # [4 5 6 7] [ 2  6 10] [[4, 5], [8, 9]]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Correction</div><p>The notebook labels slicing “DAP (Distributed Array Protocol)” — that's unrelated. Slicing is plain <code>start:stop:step</code> indexing, per axis, separated by commas.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A slice of a NumPy array is a <strong>view</strong> — changing it changes the original. Use <code>.copy()</code> when you need an independent array. (List slices, by contrast, are copies.)</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Replace Python loops over numbers with vectorised expressions: <code>arr[arr &gt; 2]</code>, <code>arr * 2</code>, <code>arr.sum()</code>.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Building NumPy arrays by appending in a loop — collect into a list first, then call <code>np.array(list)</code> once.</p></div></div></section>"}